@page "/sign-in"
@layout EmptyLayout
@*
    The components namespace has to be imported explicitly, as every other view that hosts
    one does. Without it Razor does not recognise <CreatePracticeComponent> as a component
    at all — it compiles it as an unknown HTML element, which renders absolutely nothing
    and reports no error.
*@
@using DYS.Molargo.Shared.Features.Auth.Components
@using DYS.Molargo.Shared.Features.Auth.ViewModels
@inherits MvvmComponentBase<SignInViewModel>

@*
    The prototype's sign-in screen: a two-column split with the product pitch on the left
    and the form on the right.

    The form deviates from the design on purpose. The design signs in with an email
    address; this signs in with a clinic code, a username and a password, because usernames
    are unique per clinic rather than globally — two practices can each have an "rvance",
    and an email would have to be unique across every clinic in the world to work as an
    identity.

    The design's MFA step, SSO button and "create your practice" registration are not
    built, and are named at the foot of the form rather than shown as controls that do
    nothing.

    Its own layout, with no app bar: the shell's navigation would let an unauthenticated
    visitor click into a patient list.
*@

<PageTitle>Sign in — Molargo</PageTitle>

<div class="grid min-h-screen grid-cols-1 bg-bg lg:grid-cols-2">
    <div class="flex flex-col border-divider p-8 lg:border-r-2 lg:p-12">
        <div class="font-heading text-[22px] font-extrabold">
            MOLARGO<span class="text-accent">.</span>
        </div>

        <div class="my-auto max-w-[400px] py-10">
            <h2 class="mt-0 mb-2">Dental software that runs the whole practice.</h2>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/55">
                Diary, charting, billing and claims on one patient record, across every
                site the practice runs.
            </p>

            <div class="hr my-5"></div>

            <div class="flex flex-col gap-2 text-[13px]">
                <div>— Offline-first: the surgery keeps running without a connection</div>
                <div>— One record per patient, shared across your locations</div>
                <div>— Every clinic's data isolated by its own tenant</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="text-[11px] text-ink/55">
            © 2026 Molargo · a demonstration build
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid place-items-center p-8 lg:p-12">
        <div class="w-[min(400px,100%)]">
            @*
                The design's two tabs. Sign in is the common case and stays first; creating
                a practice is a once-ever action for one person, so it does not get a route
                of its own — a bookmarkable /create-practice would be a URL nobody needs and
                one more unauthenticated page to reason about.
            *@
            <div class="seg mb-6">
                <button type="button" class="@TabCss(!_creating)"
                        @onclick="() => _creating = false">
                    Sign in
                </button>

                <button type="button" class="@TabCss(_creating)"
                        @onclick="() => _creating = true">
                    Create practice
                </button>
            </div>

            @if (_creating)
            {
                <CreatePracticeComponent Created="OnPracticeCreated" />
            }
            else
            {
            <h4 class="mt-0 mb-4">Sign in</h4>

            @if (ViewModel.HasFailure)
            {
                @*
                    A lockout is headlined differently from a wrong password, because the
                    right response to it is to wait rather than to try again. The banner
                    itself is the app's standard failure notice — the same light red a
                    refused save gets, so the one place a stranger meets this app first
                    is not also the one place it looks like a different app.
                *@
                <NoticeComponent Boxed="true" Spacing="mb-4" Kind="NoticeKind.Failure"
                                 Headline="@(ViewModel.IsLockedOut
                                     ? "Locked out." : "Not signed in.")"
                                 Message="@ViewModel.Failure" />
            }

            @*
                A real form element, so Enter submits and browsers offer to save the
                credential. Without it the password box is a dead end for anyone who does
                not reach for the mouse.
            *@
            <form class="flex flex-col gap-3.5" @onsubmit="Submit" @formname="sign-in">
                <div class="field">
                    <label for="sign-in-tenant">Clinic code</label>
                    <input id="sign-in-tenant" class="input" autocomplete="organization"
                           placeholder="e.g. molargo-dental"
                           @bind="ViewModel.TenantCode" @bind:event="oninput" />
                    <div class="mt-1 text-[11px] text-ink/55">
                        Your practice's code. Ask whoever set up the account.
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="field">
                    <label for="sign-in-username">Username</label>
                    <input id="sign-in-username" class="input" autocomplete="username"
                           @bind="ViewModel.Username" @bind:event="oninput" />
                </div>

                <div class="field">
                    <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3">
                        <label for="sign-in-password">Password</label>

                        @*
                            Where people look for it, beside the field it is about. A link
                            rather than a button inside the form: a button would submit the
                            sign-in on Enter, and the target is a real page that has to
                            survive a reload and be reachable from an email.
                        *@
                        <a class="btn btn-ghost text-xs" href="/forgot-password">
                            Forgot password?
                        </a>
                    </div>

                    <input id="sign-in-password" class="input" type="password"
                           autocomplete="current-password"
                           @bind="ViewModel.Password" @bind:event="oninput" />
                </div>


                <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary btn-block min-h-[48px]"
                        disabled="@(!ViewModel.CanSubmit)">
                    @(ViewModel.IsBusy ? "Checking…" : "Continue →")
                </button>
            </form>

            <p class="mt-5 mb-0 text-xs text-ink/55">
                @*
                    Named rather than offered. A greyed-out SSO button reads as a setting an
                    administrator can turn on, and a practice would go looking for it.
                *@
                <strong>No two-factor and no single sign-on.</strong>
                The design has both; neither is built, and there is no way to enrol an
                authenticator or connect Google Workspace. A forgotten password
                emails a six-digit code, which needs the practice's mail account
                set up under Admin → Settings.
                else
                {
                    @*
                        No self-service reset on a device install, and the reason is the
                        store rather than the plumbing: this app has no server, so a token
                        written here lives only in this device's database and a link
                        opened anywhere else finds nothing. Said plainly rather than
                        offered and broken.
                    *@
                    <span>
                        There is no emailed reset on this device — ask somebody with staff
                        access to set your password under Admin → Users. An owner can only
                        be reset by another owner.
                    </span>
                }
            </p>

            <p class="mt-2 mb-0 text-xs text-ink/55">
                @* Registration is built now — the tab above creates a practice with a
                   30-day trial. What is still missing is the rest of this list. *@
                Five wrong passwords lock an account for fifteen minutes, and every attempt
                — successful or not — is written to the audit log under Admin.
            </p>
            }
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>Which tab is showing. Sign in unless the visitor asks otherwise.</summary>
    private bool _creating;

    private static string TabCss(bool isSelected) =>
        isSelected ? "seg-opt seg-opt-on" : "seg-opt";

    /// <summary>
    /// Switches to sign-in with the new clinic's code already filled in.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The code was derived from the practice name rather than chosen, so the person who
    /// just signed up would otherwise have to retype something they have only just been
    /// shown. Their username and password they type themselves — prefilling a password
    /// field from a signup form is how one ends up in a browser's saved-form history.
    /// </remarks>
    private void OnPracticeCreated(string clinicCode)
    {
        ViewModel.TenantCode = clinicCode;
        _creating = false;
    }

    private Task Submit() =>
        ViewModel.CanSubmit ? ViewModel.SignInCommand.ExecuteAsync() : Task.CompletedTask;
}
